A||N||5||Seanh||Behind the seat amp racks||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||04-14-2001||03:39 PM||||I'm lookin for ideas on the small SQ system I am going to put in the new Fiero. (For those wondering, I bought a maroon 87GT from a friend about a month ago and have been cleaning it up and all, hasn't been driven in 3 years) Im going to put a descent system in it, guess that was a given. Going to put a ported box firing at the bottem of the front trunk and ported into the cabin. Holding 2 Image Dynamics IDQ 12's. 2 Alpine V12 amps. One 4-channel bridged into 2-channels running the front MB Quart PSC 316 3-way components. 6-1/2" Woofers in the kick's, 4" midrange and 1" tweeter in the dash. One 2-channel amp bridged into 1 mono channel running 250w RMS and 500w MAX to each sub, wired in parallel for a 2ohm mono load. Now that thats over with, I need some ideas on an amp rack to hold one amp behind each seat. I want them flush, so Im gonna build in the entire area back there, also the conceal wireing. I would like to enclose them under lightly smoked Lexan and there will be 2 mini fans on each side, to push and pull air thru and keep them cool. Anyone have any pictures of behind the seat amp racks similar to my idea? Not sure what I'm going to make it out of, probally with MDF or fiberglass, but Im still open to ideas. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Mach10||04-14-2001||07:13 PM||||You are talking about bridging a v12 to power just your front components, and this is "just a small SQ system"?!?<BR>How the HELL are you going to fix 2x12" ported subs in the front?!?<BR>That will be one HELL of an install job when it's done  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I wish I had the kind of cash to do something like that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>Good luck!||||reg||11
Z||000002||Seanh||04-14-2001||07:40 PM||||This is a pretty small system comparted to what I do all day. Ya ever seen 5 15" JL w3's in a Geo Metro hittin 171.3 dB without blowing a speaker? I have   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I guess I can just dream about that for a while until I decided to take the passenger seat out and fill it with speakers to make a fiero hit like that. 2 ported 12's in a 3.6 cuft box tuned to 28Hz via (2) 4" dia port tube will fit up there. I have already started on that. See, most folks, myself included, use a bandpass box to get the most out of the subs up front, because ALL the sound is directed thru the ports. In a ported box only around half the sound is dricted thru ports, and also makes for a smaller box requirement. That alpine V12 that will run the front stage puts out 60w RMS x 4 at 4omhs. The speakers I am using are 3 way components, so 6 speakers in a "pair". 3 speakers will be on each channel of the amp getting 120w rms into each set. The Fiero cabin is so small, and the speakers are so close together I don't see a need for any rear speakers at all. Im going to get some 84 sail panels and eliminate the speakers back there. I think they move the sound stage back to far to sound good. <P>Hey, Im usally pretty broke too. I have my money spent before I have it. This entire system will cost me less then $800, and its all brand new equipment. Heres what Im looking at cost wise:<BR>$300 - Front Components<BR>$200 - 2 Alpine V12 amps<BR>$160 - 2 Image Dynamics IDQ 12" Subs<BR>$150 - Installation Supplies, wood, wires, all that good stuff<BR>If ya know what your doing you can have a damn nice system pretty cheap. I am going to keep very detailed information on building my front subbox for anyone who is interested. I will require removeing the bottem of the spare tire pod, spare tire and jack of course, washer fluid tank, and maybe (if you have it) the A/C unit up there. I am going to try my best to work around that, but I dont know how it will work out. One thing that kinda makes me mad is my subs will have to be off to the passenger side a little because the damn brake MC is in the way, and it would be WAY to much work to change that. Might hurt my installation score in competition a little, but they would probally understand.<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 04-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula88||04-14-2001||08:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Seanh:<BR><B>This is a pretty small system comparted to what I do all day. Ya ever seen 5 15" JL w3's in a Geo Metro hittin 171.3 dB without blowing a speaker? I have    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> [This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 04-14-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>To each his own, but why would anyone want a sound system that'll hit 171.3 dB?  Moreover, I never did understand why someone would buy a cheap car, and them put twice as much money into the stereo as they did into the car.  I dare you sit in the car and honestly tell me you enjoy that.  I don't care what kind of music you're playing.  That's why I love the fact that so much in stereo competition these days revolves around clarity and staging.  Loud for it's own sake, IMHO, is a waste of time and money.  But I guess it's no different than who could shove the biggest big block with the biggest cam into their 67 Nova back in High School.  The game's changed, but I guess the goal still remains the same.  Reminds me of a friend of mine in High School who had an 83 Motly Carlo SS with $10K worth of Rockfish and Altec Lansing in it.  Ok, getting off my soapbox now.  Good luch with the system!  <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000004||Seanh||04-14-2001||08:19 PM||||Oh no, I couldn't sit in that car and listen to it. It doesn't have any other speakers. Just 5 15" subs and 5 MMAT's amp in it. It was obviously SPL only. I am not going to have a hard hitting setup in this car, thats why Im putting everthing in front of me. That will allow the entire sound stage to come from the front, which will make for a great sounding setup. I think you could fit 8 12" subs in the fiero if you wanted a complete spl setup. 4 behind the seats, 2 in the rear window, and 2 up front, probally an additional one in the foot well also. That would really do good becuase the small cabin setup. Thats why they choose a Geo Metro to do that system in, because the sound has less distance to travel then in a normal car, so your SPL score will be alot higher. Porbally why most people are perfectly content with 1 10" sub in the Fiero.<P>I do completely understand about buying a $300 car and putting a $5000 stereo in it. There is a loser at school who got is car for $100 and has us put a $900 stereo in it. He didn't want us to do anything fancy like sound matt the trunk. That thing rattles like crazy and he apperently enjoys it.<P>Just 1 more FYI. When they get the car to hit that hard, they play a test tone CD. It would never hit near that hard in regular music.<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 04-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||04-14-2001||11:20 PM||||Here's how mine is going so far. Due to the fact I need the drivers seat all the way back I am only putting the equipment on the passenger side. Once wired I am going to make a fiber board and lexan cover so all you'll see will be the tops of the equipment.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/006778.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/006778.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-14-2001).]||||reg||1
